[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswa2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the VRRP gateway for Office A (keepalived on VLAN 10) running on the distribution switches dswa1/dswa2. Mechanism: Both switches are configured with an abnormally large VRRP advertisement interval (advert_int 4). With VRRP, the Master-Down timer is roughly 3 × advert_int plus a small skew, so a missed burst of adverts or a brief control-plane hiccup causes the BACKUP to wait ~12–13 seconds before taking over. During that window, the virtual IP 10.1.0.1 may not answer, which appears as the “gateway dies for a few seconds” the users observe every few minutes whenever there’s a transient (STP/OSPF churn or brief keepalived scheduling delay). Localization and evidence: On dswa1, br0.10 holds 10.1.0.1/32 and 10.1.0.2/24; on dswa2, br0.10 holds 10.1.0.3/24 (BACKUP) — both with keepalived present. The keepalived configs on both switches explicitly set advert_int 4 in vrrp_instance VI_10. STP on access and distro is stable and pings to 10.1.0.1 are clean when tested, pointing away from L2 faults and toward control-plane failover behavior as the cause of intermittent drops. Root cause: Misconfigured VRRP advertisement interval (4 seconds) on dswa1/dswa2 for VLAN 10, causing long master-down detection and visible outages during transient events. Corrective fix: Reduce advert_int to 1s (or 1/2–1s) on VI_10 (and other VRRP instances), optionally enable a small preempt_delay and use_vmac to minimize ARP churn; then reload keepalived on dswa1 and dswa2. This will make any failover sub-second and eliminate the perceived gateway “dropouts.” 
=== END ===